\subsection*{1. Forward Process (Data to Noise)}
We use a continuous-time variance-preserving (VP) diffusion process over
$t \in [0,1]$:
\begin{equation}
    dx_t
    =
    -\frac{1}{2}\beta(t)x_t\,dt
    +
    \sqrt{\beta(t)}\,dw_t .
\end{equation}
The implementation uses a linear noise-rate schedule,
\begin{equation}
    \beta(t)
    =
    \beta_{\min}
    +
    t(\beta_{\max}-\beta_{\min}),
    \qquad
    \beta_{\min}=0.1,\quad \beta_{\max}=20 .
\end{equation}

For a clean centered sample $x_0 \sim p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)$, the forward
marginal has the form
\begin{equation}
    x_t = \mu(t)x_0 + \sigma(t)\epsilon,
    \qquad
    \epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0,I),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
    \mu(t)
    &=
    \exp
    \left[
        -\frac{1}{4}(\beta_{\max}-\beta_{\min})t^2
        -
        \frac{1}{2}\beta_{\min}t
    \right],
    \\
    \sigma^2(t)
    &=
    1
    -
    \exp
    \left[
        -\frac{1}{2}(\beta_{\max}-\beta_{\min})t^2
        -
        \beta_{\min}t
    \right].
\end{align}
The implementation centers both the input sample and sampled noise, so the
diffusion process stays in the centered coordinate subspace that training uses.

\subsection*{2. Score Matching Objective}
The score network $s_\theta(x_t,t)$ estimates the conditional score of the
Gaussian forward kernel:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla_{x_t}\log p_{0t}(x_t|x_0)
    =
    -\frac{\epsilon}{\sigma(t)} .
\end{equation}

The denoising score-matching loss uses the VP marginal variance as the
weighting function:
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{diff}}(\theta)
    &=
    \mathbb{E}_{t,x_0,\epsilon}
    \left[
        \sigma^2(t)
        \left\|
            s_\theta(x_t,t)
            -
            \nabla_{x_t}\log p_{0t}(x_t|x_0)
        \right\|_2^2
    \right]
    \nonumber \\
    &=
    \mathbb{E}_{t,x_0,\epsilon}
    \left[
        \sigma^2(t)
        \left\|
            s_\theta(x_t,t)
            +
            \frac{\epsilon}{\sigma(t)}
        \right\|_2^2
    \right].
\end{align}
The implementation clamps training times below by $10^{-5}$ and floors
$\sigma(t)$ at the corresponding positive value before forming the score target.

\subsection*{3. Reverse Generative Process (Sampling)}
Sampling integrates the reverse-time VP SDE from $t=1$ down to a small positive
time, currently $10^{-4}$:
\begin{equation}
    dx_t
    =
    \left[
        f(t)x_t
        -
        g(t)^2 s_\theta(x_t,t)
    \right]dt
    +
    g(t)d\bar{w}_t,
\end{equation}
where the linear VP coefficients satisfy
\begin{equation}
    f(t)=-\frac{1}{2}\beta(t),
    \qquad
    g(t)^2=\beta(t).
\end{equation}

Using Euler-Maruyama with positive step size $\Delta t$ for the backward step,
\begin{align}
    x_{t-\Delta t}
    &=
    x_t
    -
    \left[
        f(t)x_t
        -
        g(t)^2s_\theta(x_t,t)
    \right]\Delta t
    +
    g(t)\sqrt{\Delta t}\,z,
    \qquad
    z \sim \mathcal{N}(0,I).
\end{align}
The sampler recenters each state, clips sample score norms to $64$, checks for
non-finite samples, and omits the final stochastic noise draw on the last step.
